-- title:
--   Frobenius descent: aᵖ∈ Jₙ₊₁ implies a∈ Jₙ
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a natural number $h_0$, and let $H$ be a family of types indexed by $v\in\mathbb N$, each $H(v)$ a commutative ring carrying a Hopf algebra structure over $\mathbb{Z}/p$ whose comultiplication is cocommutative and which is finite as a $\mathbb{Z}/p$-module. Assume given bialgebra maps $s_v\colon H(v+1)\to H(v)$ over $\mathbb{Z}/p$, each surjective, such that $\dim_{\mathbb{Z}/p}H(v)=p^{v h_0}$ for all $v$, and such that $\ker s_v$ equals the $p^v$-torsion ideal of $H(v+1)$, i.e. the image under the algebra endomorphism $[p^v]$ — the $p^v$-th convolution power of the identity, converted back into an algebra map — of the augmentation ideal $\ker\varepsilon$ of $H(v+1)$; assume moreover that each $H(v)$ is a local ring. For $m\in\mathbb N$ write $J_{w,m}\subseteq H(w)$ for the ideal generated by $\{b^{p^m}: b\in\ker\varepsilon\subseteq H(w)\}$. Then for all $n,w$ with $n+1\le w$ and all $a\in H(w)$: if $a^p\in J_{w,n+1}$ then $a\in J_{w,n}$.
--
--   In geometric terms this is the statement that the Frobenius $F$ on a $p$-divisible group presented as such a tower is an epimorphism: the induced map $H(w)/J_{w,n}\to H(w)/J_{w,n+1}$ on the coordinate rings of the Frobenius kernels is injective. It feeds the computation of $\dim_{\mathbb{Z}/p} H(w)/J_{w,n}$ as a power of $p$ in [`PDivisibleGroup.finrank_quotient_span_pow_augIdeal_eq_pow_of_isLocalRing`](thm.html#PDivisibleGroup.finrank_quotient_span_pow_augIdeal_eq_pow_of_isLocalRing).
